// Test: bugfixes/always_invariant_enforced_at_method_return
// Grammar: class_body · always_block
// Semantic: A class `always:` block declares invariant expressions that MUST
//   hold immediately before every user-authored method returns. On the
//   happy path — every method leaves the object in a state that satisfies
//   the invariant — execution proceeds normally with no observable
//   difference from a class that has no `always:` block. On invariant
//   violation the method traps (WASM unreachable) instead of returning;
//   this pin covers the happy path so a regression that silently drops the
//   invariant-check emission would still show green here but the sibling
//   violation-trap pin (see below) would catch it.
//
// Enforcement wiring lives in the MIR builder:
//   src/mir/mir_builder/functions.rs:220,238-243 — per-method, seeds
//     `pending_class_invariants` from the class's TAST invariants (empty
//     in `--release` mode so checks are stripped from optimized builds).
//   src/mir/mir_builder/functions.rs:55-70 — the function build context
//     picks them up as `initial_ensure_conditions`.
//   src/mir/mir_builder/statements.rs:749-819 — every method's return
//     iterates `context.ensure_conditions`, emits a Branch on each, and
//     routes false → `MirTerminator::Trap` (WASM `unreachable`).
//
// The prompt on the errors dashboard (f501c337-836d-11f1-9d55-da25a95a496b)
//   claimed no codegen consumer existed for HirClass.invariants. That was
//   true when the prompt was filed but the ensure/invariant lowering had
//   landed in the MIR builder by 0.33.115. This pin locks the current
//   working behavior in so a future refactor of the MIR ensure-conditions
//   pathway cannot silently un-enforce class invariants.
//
// Fixed in: compiler 0.33.115
//
// Expected output:
//   Initial: 5
//   After increment: 6
//   After add 3: 9
//   Always block test passed

start:
	Counter c = Counter(5)
	print("Initial: " + c.getValue().toString())

	c.increment()
	print("After increment: " + c.getValue().toString())

	c.add(3)
	print("After add 3: " + c.getValue().toString())

	print("Always block test passed")

class Counter
	integer value

	constructor(integer initial)
		value = initial

	always:
		value >= 0

	functions:
		public:
			integer getValue()
				return value

			void increment()
				value = value + 1

			void add(integer n)
				value = value + n
